Scott: I think it probably was Mario one.

405
00:26:53,902 --> 00:26:54,185
Scott: Yeah.

406
00:26:54,791 --> 00:26:55,540
Rym: Uh, trying to destroy it.

407
00:26:55,640 --> 00:26:57,479
Rym: Well, I'm pretty sure it was Mario one where I encountered this.

408
00:26:57,842 --> 00:26:59,980
Rym: I didn't have an NES yet, but a friend of ours did.

409
00:27:00,020 --> 00:27:09,980
Rym: And we're over at their house and we're playing the games and in world one, four, they said words that messed me up for a couple of years.

410
00:27:10,301 --> 00:27:14,420
Rym: They said, Oh, every four levels, there's an ending guy you have to beat.

411
00:27:15,802 --> 00:27:20,020
Rym: And they called them ending guys and other people.

412
00:27:20,160 --> 00:27:32,878
Scott: I mean, without the communication mechanisms of the present day, you know, my cousins always called like, you know, we would call them levels, but it's like, if you looked at the game, it said world one dash four, right?

413
00:27:33,661 --> 00:27:43,020
Rym: There was a Nintendo world, a Nintendo power article about the definitions of stage board and world in relation to my cousins just said board all the time.

414
00:27:43,221 --> 00:27:50,359
Scott: Even though I said level and other people said world, I didn't hear, I never heard people actually say the word stage.

415
00:27:50,601 --> 00:27:59,400
Scott: Usually stage was referred to as like in a fighting game, choosing the background or something, you know, where it's as opposed to like a level in a Mario game.

416
00:27:59,500 --> 00:28:05,098
Rym: I feel like those terms have lost whatever more specific definitions they had and have morphed over time.

417
00:28:05,642 --> 00:28:07,560
Scott: But you know, kids couldn't communicate with each other.

418
00:28:07,740 --> 00:28:10,820
Scott: So they had regional dialects and no one was telling anyone what to say.

419
00:28:10,960 --> 00:28:19,080
Rym: So I lived in a little cluster of probably about a dozen or two dozen kids who called the ending guy ending guy in all contexts.

420
00:28:19,960 --> 00:28:24,504
Rym: And then I remember we moved to another neighborhood and I was said something, something ending guy.

421
00:28:24,584 --> 00:28:33,420
Rym: And this kid, Kevin, I remember this so specifically because that kid later stole a copy of mega man three from a local bully and sold it to my brother.

422
00:28:33,943 --> 00:28:36,280
Rym: And then the bully came looking for his game and it was all scandal.

423
00:28:36,942 --> 00:28:39,816
Rym: But anyway, I said something, something ending guy.

424
00:28:39,937 --> 00:28:42,170
Rym: and Kevin said, what the fuck is an ending guy?

425
00:28:42,733 --> 00:28:43,740
Rym: What are you talking about?

426
00:28:44,180 --> 00:28:47,904
Scott: And then I mean, you could sort of figure it out what you meant based on the context.

427
00:28:48,045 --> 00:28:49,194
Rym: This kid was a little shit.

428
00:28:49,254 --> 00:28:49,960
Rym: So yeah.

429
00:28:52,342 --> 00:28:59,040
Rym: Well, we're talking about bosses and I think, uh, Mario one, like the Koopas, the bowsers that you fought at the end of Mario.

430
00:29:01,202 --> 00:29:02,086
Rym: Like those are bosses.

431
00:29:02,126 --> 00:29:10,605
Rym: That was the thing that was a, in the early video games, the concept of a boss appeared and became universal, ubiquitous.

432
00:29:10,926 --> 00:29:18,120
Rym: Like even if people don't think about it as a mechanic, like it is well known to almost anyone who plays video games.

433
00:29:18,541 --> 00:29:23,708
Rym: And the concept is even bled into tabletop role-playing games and other types of games as well.

434
00:29:24,071 --> 00:29:27,897
Rym: Even though it's not really in sports, it's not like there aren't bosses in sports.

435
00:29:27,918 --> 00:29:32,824
Rym: Like, ah, you beat the Mets in the game and now you have to go have a fistfight with the shadow Mets.

436
00:29:33,508 --> 00:29:35,640
Scott: That would be awesome though.

437
00:29:35,740 --> 00:29:36,866
Rym: That would be awesome.

438
00:29:36,886 --> 00:29:42,631
Scott: Um, but yeah, I mean, you know, the idea of, of having a boss in a game, right?

439
00:29:42,812 --> 00:29:48,138
Scott: It's like you're playing a game, whatever the loop of that game is, is just sort of rolling, right?

440
00:29:48,218 --> 00:29:53,283
Scott: Following its patterns, increasing in difficulty, decreasing in difficulty, whatever kind of game it is.

441
00:29:53,303 --> 00:30:01,199
Scott: And then it's sort of just sort of, you know, in sort of a narrative aspect, it's like, you want to have some kind of climax, right?

442
00:30:01,219 --> 00:30:13,540
Scott: It's like, you know, no matter how your game is structured, if you don't have this sort of like, you know, big finish, like a finale to your fireworks show, it's sort of like, eh, can you, you know, it's sort of like,

443
00:30:13,700 --> 00:30:14,807
Rym: well, think about precursor games,

444
00:30:14,827 --> 00:30:16,678
Scott: play like a Pac-Man or a Dig Dug.

445
00:30:16,738 --> 00:30:19,757
Scott: It's just like, you just keep playing and playing and playing and playing.

446
00:30:19,798 --> 00:30:22,691
Rym: Let's talk about the first platformer that had an ending, Pitfall.

447
00:30:23,073 --> 00:30:28,943
Rym: Pitfall just goes steadily at the, the amount of fun and interaction you're having is linear the entire time effectively.

448
00:30:28,963 --> 00:30:31,234
Rym: So when you win, it's, that's it.

449
00:30:31,515 --> 00:30:32,460
Rym: There's no boss to fight.

450
00:30:32,621 --> 00:30:33,989
Rym: Nothing happens when you win.

451
00:30:34,010 --> 00:30:34,110
Rym: Right.

452
00:30:34,613 --> 00:30:34,714
Scott: Yeah.

453
00:30:34,734 --> 00:30:45,466
Scott: You think about like the earliest game, like a Space Invaders, it's like the, it gets faster and faster and faster and it ramps up the intensity, but there, you, it's like, it's sort of, it's very linear, right?

454
00:30:45,687 --> 00:30:47,296
Scott: Or I don't know if it might be exponential.

455
00:30:47,317 --> 00:30:49,992
Scott: I haven't looked at the numbers for Space Invaders, but either way, right?

456
00:30:50,233 --> 00:30:51,520
Scott: It's just a ramp up, right?

457
00:30:51,940 --> 00:30:57,506
Scott: It's, it's, you know, there isn't this sort of big spike in challenge or divergence, right?

458
00:30:57,546 --> 00:31:01,041
Scott: It's just the same thing the whole time and it can get boring.

459
00:31:01,885 --> 00:31:22,334
Scott: Um, and so a boss is basically just a way to say, all right, we're going to punctuate the action with something bigger, different, exciting, uh, you know, but still the same game and pays almost any kind of game that has, you know, a length to it and is especially a single player game.

460
00:31:22,656 --> 00:31:35,777
Scott: You need something like that at some point, you know, whether it's, whether, you know, most of the time it takes the form of a character that is different from the usual enemies you fight for you to fight, but it doesn't always have to take that form.

461
00:31:36,199 --> 00:31:37,645
Scott: You know, it could take some other form.

462
00:31:38,147 --> 00:31:56,842
Rym: I mean, the, the boss, what, what game designers call the boss today of a level or a stage or a board is, is basically just the climax encounter of that unit of the game that is in some way materially different from everything that led up to that point, but it could still take the same form.

463
00:31:56,862 --> 00:31:57,746
Rym: I take a different form.

464
00:31:58,128 --> 00:32:07,901
Rym: According to Wikipedia, the first video game to feature a boss was a game released for the Plato system in 1975

465
00:32:07,901 --> 00:32:09,666
Scott: called, I've never

466
00:32:09,706 --> 00:32:19,156
Rym: heard of this either, but the game was called D and D and it was basically a bastardized version of Dungeons and Dragons in

467
00:32:19,357 --> 00:32:19,437
Scott: the

468
00:32:20,380 --> 00:32:23,273
Rym: tutor programming language for the Plato system.

469
00:32:23,313 --> 00:32:26,106
Rym: And I'm going to learn more about this after.

470
00:32:26,588 --> 00:32:40,722
Scott: Well, you could say that like D and D campaigns were some of the first games to have, you know, where you didn't have to have bosses, but you typically would structure your campaign about like, okay, here's whatever, a Vecna.

471
00:32:40,863 --> 00:32:43,074
Scott: And it's like, you fight all kinds of other nasties.

472
00:32:43,194 --> 00:32:47,030
Scott: And then at the end of your campaign, you fight the Vecna, right.

473
00:32:47,592 --> 00:32:49,520
Scott: As your, your climax of your story.

474
00:32:49,740 --> 00:33:15,616
Rym: Well, if you go before video games and I'm skimming and while Wikipedia actually says something similar, but it goes very specific, Wikipedia points at Bruce Lee's movies as introducing the concept of bosses, but they specifically cite in the first sentence, his 1971 film, The Big Boss, where he fights a bunch of gang members and then he fights the boss of the gang members.

475
00:33:15,657 --> 00:33:16,882
Rym: That's basically the whole movie.

476
00:33:17,565 --> 00:33:26,909
Scott: Yeah, but it had to be, I mean, there are kung fu movies older than Bruce Lee, and I feel like they all had that same kind of structure where there's, you know, all these other fights.

477
00:33:26,969 --> 00:33:29,702
Scott: And then at the end, you have to fight the strongest guy.

478
00:33:29,883 --> 00:33:44,860
Scott: Maybe that's just the term boss came from there, but they had that structure, the narrative structure of fighting your way to the top and then fighting the biggest guy at the end existed, had to exist before Bruce Lee.

479
00:33:44,880 --> 00:33:46,446
Scott: I can't imagine that was the first one.

480
00:33:46,466 --> 00:33:46,847
Scott: Yeah.

481
00:33:47,148 --> 00:33:52,909
Rym: I mean, well, 1971 is the year of that movie, so I don't know what year.

482
00:33:52,970 --> 00:34:00,737
Rym: different films like that that I like came out without looking them up, but that concept had to be in the 60s.

483
00:34:01,740 --> 00:34:19,116
Rym: Yeah, but that concept is really solid and it's really interesting to me that in the earliest era of video games, like Mario 1 had that very specific structure of every level, you know, one, two, one, three, one, one, one, two, one, three, one, four, and then you would fight a boss that was a bigger monster.

484
00:34:19,136 --> 00:34:28,400
Rym: The screen would stop scrolling and you were just on a screen interacting with this one monster, and this became such an ubiquitous pattern in all game design sense.

485
00:34:29,967 --> 00:34:42,828
Scott: So I think, you know, what made bosses exciting, especially in the early days, but also now, is that sometimes the boss fight is like a different thing than the rest of the game, right?

486
00:34:43,871 --> 00:34:54,674
Scott: Or is played differently and is almost like a sometimes a completely different game than, you know, or at least even if it's the same game, it's still played significantly differently.

487
00:34:54,694 --> 00:35:01,560
Rym: I remember I had a side scrolling component, but then you'd have a boss fight periodically.

488
00:35:01,680 --> 00:35:04,571
Rym: that was a light gun style game instead.

489
00:35:04,591 --> 00:35:06,939
Scott: Yeah, or like in a shmup, right?

490
00:35:06,979 --> 00:35:21,509
Scott: It's like, okay, you're still a ship, you're still shooting upwards, but the boss is, all right, you're not, you don't, instead of having these guys coming at you in waves and whatnot, you're now just shooting this thing at the top of the screen and trying to hit it in its weak spots.

491
00:35:21,992 --> 00:35:23,198
Scott: It's taking up the whole screen.

492
00:35:23,239 --> 00:35:37,888
Scott: It's not going anywhere, and, you know, it's going to blast you with who knows how much stuff that you have to dodge intricately, and it's, that's very different than the other part of the game where guys are coming at you ones and twos at a time, and they die sort of instantly.

493
00:35:37,908 --> 00:35:43,853
Scott: You're not pumping a lot into one spot or trying to figure out which spot to shoot or anything like that.

494
00:35:43,873 --> 00:36:14,600
Scott: You know, it's, you know, it's different, or as in other games, maybe like, um, like, uh, Alfred Chicken, for example, is like a, you know, I just always remember because I love the bosses in that one, even though they're kind of stupid, but it's like you would, the game was a puzzle platformer, but when you got to the boss of the levels, it was sort of like, you know, uh, like a little shmup for your sort of, you know, going around the screen, trying to shoot this guy who's coming after you, and it was nothing like the rest of the game, completely different.

495
00:36:15,382 --> 00:36:25,924
Scott: The other thing that's exciting about the bosses, especially when you're kids with simpler technology, is that just the, the novelty of seeing a thing that is different, right?

496
00:36:26,004 --> 00:36:35,329
Scott: It's like you're, you're playing, you know, Zelda one, and it's like, yeah, every screen just has these little guys that are one square each with different sprites, right?

497
00:36:35,852 --> 00:36:41,599
Scott: And then you go to see a boss and it's this big thing, and it's like these sprites that you don't see anywhere else.

498
00:36:42,401 --> 00:36:48,034
Scott: Someone put together this whole section of the sprite sheet for the game, and you can only see those sprites in this one room.

499
00:36:48,094 --> 00:36:49,422
Scott: It's like, you want to see Ganon?

500
00:36:49,663 --> 00:36:50,508
Scott: He's in this one room.

501
00:36:50,528 --> 00:36:52,339
Scott: That's the only way you can ever see the Ganon.

502
00:36:52,860 --> 00:37:01,239
Scott: And so just like seeing that image when you didn't have pictures of it or an internet to look up sprite sheets, or it's like, you get to see it again.

503
00:37:01,259 --> 00:37:11,178
Rym: And only if you can get to that room and usually take a photo of it with your, they had something like in final, in the final fantasy legend two game.

504
00:37:11,198 --> 00:37:14,461
Rym: I remember, you know, the, the, the monster characters that would eat meat to level up.

505
00:37:14,862 --> 00:37:19,920
Rym: Sometimes you'd fight a boss, like a named story boss, and they drop meat.

506
00:37:20,541 --> 00:37:29,949
Rym: And I got to tell you as a kid without the internet, when a boss drops meat that your character can eat and transform, you expect something good is going to happen when you eat that meat.

507
00:37:29,989 --> 00:37:31,117
Rym: Like, that's really exciting.

508
00:37:31,519 --> 00:37:32,870
Rym: Like, what's my, what's my pet going to do?

509
00:37:32,910 --> 00:37:34,441
Rym: Is it going to turn into a dragon or something?

510
00:37:34,862 --> 00:37:36,026
Rym: Dungeon mashy style?

511
00:37:38,914 --> 00:37:49,559
Rym: But the boss fights, all the bosses tend to, like we've, we've developed this lexicon of talking about bosses in games to the point that a lot of people don't think about where these terms came from.

512
00:37:49,619 --> 00:38:00,808
Rym: Like, I've definitely encountered people who refer to mini bosses and square bosses, but they don't know where mini bosses came from or that square boss is literally from the name square enix.

513
00:38:01,471 --> 00:38:01,632
Scott: Yeah.

514
00:38:01,672 --> 00:38:02,315
Scott: Mini bosses.

515
00:38:02,375 --> 00:38:04,606
Scott: I usually associate with mega man and Kirby.

516
00:38:04,626 --> 00:38:07,540
Scott: I mean, mega man is a game that's only about bosses, right?

517
00:38:07,802 --> 00:38:15,896
Scott: Kirby is also about bosses, but less so than mega man, but in Kirby game was a, was a mini boss in dragon, uh, double dragon.

518
00:38:15,916 --> 00:38:16,639
Rym: He'd show up.

519
00:38:16,659 --> 00:38:17,384
Rym: You'd have to fight him.

520
00:38:17,645 --> 00:38:19,135
Rym: He was different from everybody else.

521
00:38:19,175 --> 00:38:21,005
Scott: The beat them ups are also about bosses.

522
00:38:21,367 --> 00:38:27,494
Scott: Um, but mega man, literally the main gate, the stage select screen of mega man is here are the boss.

523
00:38:27,554 --> 00:38:28,298
Rym: Here's the boss.

524
00:38:28,358 --> 00:38:30,168
Rym: Click here to go through his level to fight.

525
00:38:30,208 --> 00:38:33,325
Scott: The game is about fighting bosses and taking their powers.

526
00:38:33,406 --> 00:38:36,423
Scott: That is, you know, it says no more boss game than mega man is.

527
00:38:36,824 --> 00:38:40,056
Scott: Um, but in Kirby, there's a lot of mini bosses.

528
00:38:40,197 --> 00:38:43,229
Scott: It's like, okay, yeah, the boss is wispy woods, right?

529
00:38:43,550 --> 00:38:48,216
Scott: Which is, you know, again, this big thing that's different from all the other levels, right?

530
00:38:48,256 --> 00:38:56,354
Scott: You know, you're fighting a one guy instead of side scrolling, but each level would have like frosty here or the guy with the symbols on his shoulders.

531
00:38:56,415 --> 00:38:57,600
Scott: I forget his name, right?

532
00:38:57,861 --> 00:39:02,405
Scott: We're like, you'd have to stop on this one screen or the big wheel that just drives back and forth.

533
00:39:02,927 --> 00:39:12,993
Scott: It's like, yeah, there's normal enemies all over, but on this screen you have to stop and you can't proceed and see you beat this one guy who's a little bit bigger than all the other normal bad guys.

534
00:39:13,996 --> 00:39:16,309
Scott: Um, and so those are the mini bosses, right?

535
00:39:16,329 --> 00:39:22,800
Scott: They're not the boss of the whole world, but they're still, compared to your normal gameplay, boss-like.

536
00:39:23,140 --> 00:39:27,520
Rym: I would argue mini bosses separate levels into effectively stages.

537
00:39:28,440 --> 00:39:34,060
Scott: Yeah, yeah, you know, but, um, depending on if they let you checkpoint or whatever, who knows how the game is structured.

538
00:39:35,020 --> 00:39:39,948
Rym: The square boss was the concept that I've always loved and I encountered it in Final Fantasy IV.

539
00:39:40,290 --> 00:39:42,180
Rym: Like that was when I recognized that as a thing.

540
00:39:42,800 --> 00:39:49,572
Scott: We called it the square boss because it came from mostly in games created by Square, the company that made Final Fantasy.

541
00:39:49,934 --> 00:40:02,069
Scott: And what they would do is just about every Final Fantasy game, I think literally all of them, possibly, but also many others, you would think you're fighting the final boss of the game, but then, ha ha ha ha ha.

542
00:40:02,250 --> 00:40:03,959
Scott: Actually, there is another boss.

543
00:40:04,960 --> 00:40:06,489
Scott: You thought Zemus knows Zeromas.

544
00:40:09,360 --> 00:40:11,989
Scott: Right, but even Mega Man again, right?

545
00:40:12,491 --> 00:40:14,598
Scott: Mega Man 2, it's like you beat Dr.

546
00:40:15,060 --> 00:40:18,852
Scott: Wily and then another skull, right?

547
00:40:18,972 --> 00:40:21,239
Scott: It's the alien guy, right?

548
00:40:21,319 --> 00:40:23,348
Scott: It's like really just a robot controlled by Dr.

549
00:40:23,388 --> 00:40:28,970
Scott: Wily, but it's still another boss after what you thought was the final, final boss.

550
00:40:29,553 --> 00:40:35,803
Scott: And that's a really great pattern, I think, especially in older games where people don't, didn't know that that was going to happen.

551
00:40:35,823 --> 00:40:43,039
Rym: because like, oh, the first time that happened to me in Mega Man 2 as a kid, that was, that was a fucking situation, right?

552
00:40:43,079 --> 00:40:44,928
Scott: It's like, you think you've beaten the game.

553
00:40:44,948 --> 00:40:46,838
Scott: You're like, yes, yes, I did it.

554
00:40:46,878 --> 00:40:48,806
Scott: This, like, this has to be the end, right?

555
00:40:48,846 --> 00:40:49,649
Scott: I'm at the skull.

556
00:40:49,669 --> 00:40:50,793
Scott: I beat Dr.

557
00:40:50,834 --> 00:40:51,255
Scott: Wily.

558
00:40:51,878 --> 00:40:52,460
Scott: Credits roll.

559
00:40:52,560 --> 00:40:53,002
Scott: Let's go.

560
00:40:53,103 --> 00:40:53,465
Scott: Let's go.

561
00:40:53,485 --> 00:40:55,012
Scott: And then it's like, nope.

562
00:40:55,817 --> 00:40:56,259
Scott: It's not over yet.

563
00:40:56,279 --> 00:40:59,676
Rym: Especially because the first time that happened to me, I died immediately.

564
00:40:59,756 --> 00:41:00,963
Rym: I just got crushed.

565
00:41:01,304 --> 00:41:02,934
Rym: Like, I couldn't make it past the first skull.

566
00:41:03,416 --> 00:41:04,842
Rym: The first, like, the final anything.

567
00:41:05,223 --> 00:41:05,745
Rym: I was done.

568
00:41:05,785 --> 00:41:06,026
Rym: Yep.

569
00:41:07,029 --> 00:41:07,209
Scott: Yep.

570
00:41:08,553 --> 00:41:08,654
Scott: Ah.

571
00:41:09,436 --> 00:41:12,647
Scott: So what makes a, what makes a good boss at what makes a shitty boss?

572
00:41:12,807 --> 00:41:19,771
Rym: A boss has got to have some sort of special mechanic and it can't just be random for me.

573
00:41:20,153 --> 00:41:31,657
Rym: Like you gotta, you've gotta be able to, from the game itself, the context, the game provides you figure out the boss's deal by some means other than just trial and error.

574
00:41:32,019 --> 00:41:33,947
Rym: That's, that's one of the main requirements for me.

575
00:41:35,212 --> 00:41:37,582
Scott: I think a boss has to have a deal, right?

576
00:41:37,662 --> 00:41:42,705
Scott: It can't just be, you fight me the same way that you fight all the normal bad guys.

577
00:41:42,866 --> 00:41:45,138
Scott: I just am bigger and I take more hits or whatever.

578
00:41:45,158 --> 00:41:45,259
Rym: Yeah.

579
00:41:45,279 --> 00:41:49,014
Rym: The worst boss in any game is, uh, what's his name?

580
00:41:49,455 --> 00:41:50,519
Rym: Who is not Castro?

581
00:41:50,559 --> 00:41:56,469
Rym: because you're playing as Castro in, uh, Guerrilla War, the NES game that was rebranded in the US to not be about that.

582
00:41:56,891 --> 00:42:01,942
Rym: At the end, the dictator is standing up on a tower after you beat the regular boss and now you have to fight him.

583
00:42:01,962 --> 00:42:05,498
Rym: He just stands there and he has no special deal.

584
00:42:05,538 --> 00:42:08,518
Rym: You just have to shoot him for like five minutes straight before he dies and that's it.

585
00:42:09,440 --> 00:42:10,324
Scott: Yeah, that sucks, right?

586
00:42:10,685 --> 00:42:12,473
Scott: Whereas you look at say, Zelda, right?

587
00:42:12,593 --> 00:42:14,943
Scott: Goma, perfect boss design, right?

588
00:42:14,983 --> 00:42:20,584
Scott: It's like, yes, you have to shoot me in my open eyeball with your arrow, right?

589
00:42:20,604 --> 00:42:22,151
Scott: To kill the Goma spider.

590
00:42:22,352 --> 00:42:26,611
Scott: It's like, it's like you have to use a particular item in a particular way.

591
00:42:26,952 --> 00:42:30,827
Scott: There isn't really any other bad guy that works that way, right?

592
00:42:32,072 --> 00:42:38,161
Rym: And every boss in Zelda is pretty unique, but then you meet Aqua Menace again, but he's just a mini boss when you meet him the second time.

593
00:42:38,844 --> 00:42:50,160
Scott: Well, you know, but Aqua Menace isn't, like, yet, like, he is a little different than a normal guy because he's big and he, you know, his pattern is different, but you can just fight him like a normal guy just standing there.

594
00:42:50,300 --> 00:43:01,311
Rym: True, but it, that works for me because there are so many different bosses in the, in Zelda 1 that every boss has its own deal, so it's okay that one of the bosses' deal is I'm big and I hit you.

595
00:43:01,331 --> 00:43:08,604
Scott: Well, I think all the, I think if you look at later Zelda games, not two, but the ones after that, all the bosses have a deal, right?

596
00:43:08,624 --> 00:43:18,150
Scott: You, especially, like, Link to the Past, you get the, the Helmasaur, it's like, you have to use the hammer to smash his face mask armor off and then you can attack him normally, right?

597
00:43:18,451 --> 00:43:24,136
Scott: You know, or like, there's the, the, the Misery Mire has the eyeball thing, what the hell's its name, I forget.

598
00:43:24,236 --> 00:43:26,709
Rym: Oh god, I hate, I love and hate fighting that thing.

599
00:43:27,353 --> 00:43:39,108
Scott: Yeah, and it's like, yeah, you can't get near it because it's got this pool of poison all around it, you know, it's like, or, you know, that's how you got to make a boss, it has to have something, you know, clever and different to it.

600
00:43:39,148 --> 00:43:48,900
Rym: Well, here is a good example, so Bionic Commando, the original, fantastic game, but the bosses were garbage, just, they were worthless, unfun, no good mechanics, like, nothing going on there.

601
00:43:49,400 --> 00:43:59,100
Rym: Bionic Commando Rearmed replaced those bosses with radically unique, mechanically distinct puzzles to solve, that were effectively bosses.

602
00:44:04,252 --> 00:44:42,810
Rym: Yeah, I don't want to turn this into just a list of all the good bosses we've faced over the years, but I would say actually, Final Fantasy IV had pretty good bosses, in the sense that every boss in that game was also, at least in the Japanese version, kind of a puzzle, that was consistent with the theme of the boss as a character, and had enough visual and textual tells and explanation and context, to where you could figure it out as a player, and it was kind of a puzzle, like, if you just held A and did attack with high levels, that really wouldn't actually beat those bosses, but if you were clever, you could beat him without grinding.

603
00:44:47,970 --> 00:44:51,783
Rym: Animal Well doesn't really have bosses so much, except, I mean, it

604
00:44:51,803 --> 00:45:08,770
Scott: does, it only has bosses, because they got rid of most of the normal things, right, but you're not, you're just not fighting them in a direct sense, right, is, you know, the bosses are just sort of puzzles like everything else, but they're sort of like, you know, the ending rooms of the areas.

605
00:45:10,230 --> 00:45:25,298
Rym: I guess what I really like, too, is when, like, bosses are best when they also, they've got, whatever the music is in your game, no matter how good it is, the bosses have to have better music, the bosses have to have good, special music, that, that's one of the keys to a good boss.

606
00:45:25,338 --> 00:45:26,062
Rym: in a modern game,

607
00:45:26,585 --> 00:45:54,613
Scott: like, yeah, I mean, like we said, about the purpose of the boss is to have this sort of climactic narrative, you know, intense moment, and if you don't emphasize that by changing up the music or, you know, and the, and the scene and all this stuff, it's like, well, you're really falling short of, you know, what you could be doing, right, if the regular music just keeps playing, you know, it's not as impactful, you know, emotionally or anything, so.

608
00:45:54,713 --> 00:45:55,115
Scott: The thing

609
00:45:55,175 --> 00:46:22,752
Rym: I do think is very, very good that many games do, but more so now than they used to, is the trope of, there's a boss that's very hard that you beat earlier in the game, and then way later in the game, that boss appears as a regular-ass enemy, like, all over the place, and then the boss you meet after that is so much bigger or more dangerous than any boss you've encountered, like, that's a good way to escalate the power level of a game in a fun way.

610
00:46:23,876 --> 00:47:20,350
Scott: Also a way to save sprites, you know, you don't, didn't just use it once, use it more times, but also it helps you sort of emphasize the, you know, the epicness, right, of something, you know, where it's like, you know, you have normal bad guys, and then you have boss right after that, you know, and humans are very good at measuring things relatively, and our bodies are literally built that way, like, if you put your hand in some water that's ice cold, and then move it to some lukewarm water, it feels really hot, like you're, like it's burning you, because your body is, senses things relatively, so if you have normal guys next to a regular boss, and then you put normal guys next to the mega boss, the mega boss feels like a regular boss, but if you put normal boss from level one, and then immediately, and then fight the mega boss, it's like, whoa, look how big this is compared to what I thought was big, right, whoa, this is an insane boss.

